meta {
  name: Create Execution
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/workflows/:workflowId/executions
  body: json
  auth: none
}

params:path {
  workflowId: {{workflowId}}
}

headers {
  Idempotency-Key: {{$randomUUID}}
  x-correlation-id: {{$randomUUID}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "workflowVersionId": "{{versionId}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.status === 201) {
    bru.setVar("executionId", res.body.executionId);
  }
}

docs {
  # Create Execution

  Starts one execution and returns immediately with its `executionId`. The execution is
  created as `CREATED`. In the same transaction, one `node_execution` per node in the
  version is snapshotted (all `PENDING`), and one `outbox_message` row (`PENDING`) records
  the `StartWorkflowExecution` job to dispatch. The outbox relay publishes that row as a
  BullMQ job, and the executor runs the nodes in order: the execution moves
  `CREATED → RUNNING → COMPLETED`. Poll **Get Execution** and **Get Execution History** to
  watch it.

  ## x-correlation-id
  Optional. A valid UUID is kept and stored on the outbox row and in the job payload;
  anything else is replaced with a generated UUID. The value used is echoed back in the
  response header.

  Each send generates a new value (`{{$randomUUID}}`); read the one used from the
  `x-correlation-id` response header, then search the API, relay and executor logs for it.

  The version must be `PUBLISHED` (`409` if still `DRAFT`).

  ## Idempotency-Key
  Each send generates a new key, so each send creates a new execution. Repeated requests
  with the **same** key return the existing execution instead: to see that, replace
  `{{$randomUUID}}` with a fixed value and send twice.
}
